.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt EXABGP.CONF 5
.OS
.Sh NAME
.Nm exabgp.conf
.Nd Configuration file controlling the BGP configuration for
.Xr exabgp 1 .
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
specifies the BGP neighbor configuration for exabgp.
.Ss CONFIGURATION FILE SYNTAX
.Pp
.Bd -literal -offset 3m
template {
    neighbor <template-name> {
        <neighbor-configuration>;
        // Suitable for setting common attributes
        // shared by more than one actual neighbor.

        // Can use basically any of the attributes
	// in the below neighbor configuration
    }
}

// Most attributes / settings are optional

neighbor <ip-address> {
    description <text>;
    inherit <template-name>; // from previously defined template

    router-id <router-id>;
    host-name <hostname>;
    domain-name <domain-name>;

    local-address <ip-address>;
    local-as <asn>;
    peer-as <asn>;
    hold-time <seconds>;
    rate-limit <enable | disable>;
    manual-eor <truthvalue>;

    passive <truthvalue>;
    listen <port>;   // local TCP port, default 179
    connect <port>;  // remote TCP port, default 179
    source-interface <interface-name>;

    incoming-ttl <ttl>;  // GTSM: lowest TTL we accept from the peer
    outgoing-ttl <ttl>;  // the TTL we send with

    group-updates <truthvalue>;
    auto-flush <truthvalue>;
    adj-rib-in <truthvalue>;
    adj-rib-out <truthvalue>;

    md5-password <md5-secret>;
    md5-base64 <true | false>;
    md5-ip <ip-address>;

    capability {
        add-path <disable | send | receive | send/receive>;
        asn4 <enable | disable>;
        graceful-restart <time-in-seconds>;
        multi-session <enable | disable>;
        operational <enable | disable>;
        route-refresh <enable | disable>;
        extended-message <enable | disable>;
    }

    role {
        local <provider | customer | peer | rs | rs-client>;
        strict <enable | disable>; // default disable
        add-meta <enable | disable>; // default enable
    }

    family {
        all; // default, or a list of the below
        minimal; // use AFI/SAFI required to announce routes in config
        ipv4 unicast;
        ipv4 multicast;
        ipv4 nlri-mpls;
        ipv4 mpls-vpn;
        ipv4 flow;
        ipv4 flow-vpn;
        ipv4 rtc; // RFC 4684 route target membership
        ipv6 unicast;
        ipv6 flow;
        ipv6 flow-vpn;
    }

    nexthop {
        ipv4 unicast ipv6;
        ipv4 multicast ipv6;
        ipv4 mpls-vpn ipv6;
        ipv4 nlri-mpls ipv6;
        ipv6 unicast ipv4;
        ipv6 multicast ipv4;
        ipv6 mpls-vpn ipv4;
        ipv6 nlri-mpls ipv4;
    }

    add-path {
        ipv4 unicast;
        ipv4 multicast;
        ipv4 nlri-mpls;
        ipv4 mpls-vpn;
        ipv4 flow;
        ipv4 flow-vpn;
        ipv6 unicast;
        ipv6 flow;
        ipv6 flow-vpn;
    }

    api {
        processes [ <process-name-list> ];
    }

    static {
        route <ip-address>/<prefixlength> {
          next-hop <ip-address>; // only mandatory attribute
          origin ( IGP | EGP | INCOMPLETE );
          as-path [ <as-sequence-asn> | ( <as-set ) ];
          as-sequence;
          med <med>;
          aigp <aigp>;
          local-preference <preference>;
          otc <asn | self | provider | customer | peer | rs | rs-client>;
          atomic-aggregate;
          aggregator <asn>:<ip-address>;
          path-information <ip-address>;
          community ( <community> | [ <community> <community> ... ] );
          large-community ( <large-community> | [ <large-community> ... ] );
          originator-id <ip-address>;
          cluster-list ( <ip-address> | [ <ip-address> <ip-address> ... ] );
          extended-community ( <extended-community> |
                               [ <extended-community> ... ] );
          split /<prefixlength>;
          label ( <label> | [ <label> <label> ... ] );
          rd  <route-distinguisher>;
          watchdog <watchdog-name>;
          bgp-prefix-sid ( [<label-index-value>] |
             [ <label-index-value>, [ ( <srgb-base_1>,<srgb-range_1> ), ... ] ]);
          withdraw;
        }
        // or alternatively
        route <ip-address>/<prefixlength> <attribute-sequence>;
        // where <attribute-sequence> is a sequence of
        // any of the attributes above

        // RFC 4684 route target membership (family ipv4 rtc)
        rtc origin-as <asn> route-target <asn:number | ip-address:number>
            next-hop <ip-address | self> <attribute-sequence>;
        rtc default next-hop <ip-address | self> <attribute-sequence>;
    }

    flow {
        route <route-name> {
            rd <route-distinguisher>;
            next-hop <ip-address>; // for redirect-to-nexthop
            match {
                // one or more match terms
                source <ip-address>/<prefixlength>;
                destination <ip-address>/<prefixlength>;
                port <portnumber>;
                source-port <portnumber>;
                destination-port <portnumber-expression>;
                protocol [ udp | tcp ]; // IPv4 only
                next-header [ udp | tcp ]; // IPv6 only
                tcp-flags [ fin | syn | rst | push | ack | urg | ece | cwr | ns ];
                icmp-type [ echo-reply | echo-request | info-reply |
                            info-request | mask-reply | mask-request |
                            parameter-problem | redirect | router-advertisment |
                            router-solicit | source-quench | time-exceeded |
                            timestamp | timestamp-reply | unreachable ];
                icmp-code [ communication-prohibited-by-filtering |
                            destination-host-prohibited |
                            destination-host-unknown |
                            destination-network-unknown |
                            fragmentation-needed | host-precedence-violation |
                            ip-header-bad | network-unreachable |
                            network-unreachable-for-tos | port-unreachable |
                            redirect-for-host | redirect-for-network |
                            redirect-for-tos-and-host |
                            redirect-for-tos-and-net |
                            required-option-missing | source-host-isolated |
                            source-route-failed |
                            ttl-eq-zero-during-reassembly |
                            ttl-eq-zero-during-transit ];
                fragment [ dont-fragment | is-fragment |
                           first-fragment | last-fragment ];
                // fragment is IPv4 only, poorly tested
                dscp <dscp-value>;
                traffic-class <traffic-class>;
                packet-length <packet-length-expression>;
                flow-label <flow-label-expression>; // IPv6 only
            }
            then {
                // one action only
                accept;
                discard;
                rate-limit <ratelimit>;
                redirect ( <route-distinguisher> | <ip-address> | [<ipv6-address>]:<number> );
                redirect-to-nexthop; // Ref. next-hop above
                redirect-to-nexthop-ietf <ip-address>;
                copy <ip-address>;
                mark <mark>;
                action ( sample | terminal | sample-terminal );
                community;
                large-community;
                extended-community;
            }
        }
    }

    l2vpn {
        vpls <site-name> {
            endpoint ( <vpls-endpoint> (integer) );
            offset ( <block-offset> (integer) );
            size ( <block-size> (integer) );
            base ( <label-base> (integer) );
            name <route-name>;

            next-hop <ip-address>;
            origin ( IGP | EGP | INCOMPLETE );
            as-path [ <as-sequence-asn> | ( <as-set ) ];
            med <med>;
            local-preference <preference>;

            rd <route-distinguisher>;
            community ( <community> | [ <community> <community> ... ] );
            large-community ( <large-community> |
                            [ <large-community> ... ] );

            originator-id <ip-address>;
            cluster-list [ <ip-address> <ip-address> ... ];
            // l2info: encaps:control-flag:mtu:site-preference
            extended-community ( <extended-community> |
                                 [ <extended-community> ... ] );
            withdraw;
        }
    }
}

process <process-name> {
    run <command>;
    encoding <text | json>;
}

.Ed
.Pp
Please note that multi-line sections really do need to be written as
multiple lines: the first one ending in "{", and ending with a line
with a "}" by itself.
.Sh ONLY TO CUSTOMER
The optional role block names our local eBGP relationship, not the remote role.
It requires explicit, unequal local-as and peer-as values.
Strict mode rejects a peer that omits the Role capability; otherwise its
complementary role is inferred. Incompatible advertised roles reject the OPEN.
.Pp
Static routes and API announcements accept an OTC ASN in decimal or dotted form.
The attribute always carries four octets, even with a two-octet-ASN peer.
For example, "otc 1.1" is emitted and reported as ASN 65537.
The "otc self" form resolves the announcing session's effective local ASN,
including local-as auto, without modifying the stored route.
A role name resolves the same ASN and additionally asserts that the neighbor's
configured local role matches it; a missing or different role is an error.
.Pp
Without a route-level OTC instruction, role-based marking adds the local ASN
when sending IPv4 or IPv6 unicast to a customer, peer, or route-server client.
There is no way to turn that off. RFC 9234 section 5 says the operator MUST NOT
have the ability to modify these procedures, so the "role otc" sub-option and the
route-level "otc none" instruction were removed in 6.0.0; a configuration using
either is refused with a message naming the RFC. Other address families are
untouched, as the same section requires. Existing explicit OTC values are
preserved.
.Pp
A route already carrying OTC is refused toward a provider, peer, or route
server. This applies to explicit "otc self" and role-named forms as well.
Replacing an advertised route with a refused route withdraws the old advertisement.
Withdrawals are never refused. Non-unicast families bypass these policy rules,
while preserving explicit OTC.
.Pp
Inline "exabgp encode" accepts a numeric OTC. Resolving "self"
or a role name requires "exabgp encode -c" with a real configuration.
For "otc self", offline encoding with "local-as auto" requires a concrete
"peer-as"; if both are automatic, encoding reports a configuration error.
Named OTC appears as a numeric "otc" member in decoded attribute JSON.
.Pp
Role and strict-mode changes restart the session. Changing add-meta does not.
.Pp
Received unicast leaks are logged and annotated on affected announcement entries
under "meta.route-leak". Disabling add-meta hides the annotation, not the warning
or outbound refusal. Malformed OTC uses RFC 7606 treat-as-withdraw.
Leak and export-refusal warnings use the normally enabled reactor logging category;
parser and RIB debug logging need not be enabled. Global logging and level filters
still apply.
This is partial RFC 9234 support: ExaBGP does not insert missing ingress OTC.
Helpers redistributing received routes must perform ingress marking and exclude
ineligible routes. The receive and send/receive marking settings are rejected.

.Sh GENERALIZED TTL SECURITY
GTSM (RFC 5082) is two settings on a session socket, and
.Nm
has one keyword for each.
.Ic incoming-ttl
is the lowest TTL the kernel will accept from the peer, so a packet forged
from off-link arrives with a TTL too low and is dropped before
.Xr exabgp 1
sees it.
.Ic outgoing-ttl
is the TTL we send with, and it also serves as the multihop setting it
predates.
.Pp
Both now apply to a session whichever end opened it.
Until 6.0.0 a session this side initiated installed
.Ic outgoing-ttl
and no inbound check at all, and a session the peer opened inherited a
sending TTL equal to the minimum we accept, where section 3 of the RFC has a
GTSM sender use 255.
An accepted connection takes the sending TTL of the neighbor it matched:
.Ic outgoing-ttl
when it is configured, else 255 when
.Ic incoming-ttl
is, else the kernel default.
For the usual
.Dq incoming-ttl 255
that is what passive sessions already sent, so a working GTSM pair is
unaffected.
.Pp
The inbound check needs a per-platform socket option.
It is installed on Linux and FreeBSD for both IPv4 and IPv6.
macOS has no IPv4 equivalent, so
.Nm
warns and brings the session up with the outbound TTL only: on that platform
IPv4
.Ic incoming-ttl
is not enforced.
.Pp
The listening socket is shared by every neighbor on one address and port, so
neighbors behind it configured with different
.Ic incoming-ttl
values get whichever was set last.
Give a neighbor needing its own minimum its own listening address.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr exabgp 1 .
.Sh CAVEATS
Default values ought to be documented.
The functionality and semantics of the process / api ought to be documented.
The healthcheck module of exabgp is perhaps deserved of its own man page.
